        "description": "<div id=\"link_wrapped_content\">\n<body><section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>Yesterday, after months of hand-wringing, Valve finally revealed the price of its second-generation Steam Machine. <a href=\"https://www.pcworld.com/article/3172143/the-steam-machine-finally-has-a-price-and-date-1049-june-25th.html\">It\u2019s $1,049</a>, several hundred dollars more than just about everyone was expecting when it was first previewed last year. It\u2019s the worst time to build or sell a gaming PC in decades right now, but you can still do a little better if you hunt for a bit.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Given that Valve is intending to expand its Linux-based SteamOS to self-built hardware, and you\u2019ve got plenty of other options like Bazzite besides, I decided to see if I could leverage those Amazon Prime Day deals into an equivalent desktop PC build that costs less. And I did, <a href=\"https://pcpartpicker.com/list/\">with a little help from PCPartPicker</a>. Picking an",
